.TH VSTREAMTEST 1
.SH NAME
vstreamtest \- test streaming video decode end to end
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vstreamtest
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Vstreamtest
exercises the whole streaming path:
.IR quicktime (2)
locates the coded samples in Limbo, and
.IR draw (3)'s
decoder turns them into frames in an ordinary draw image.
.PP
Nothing is staged and no path is handed to the host.
The container is read through the namespace \(em so it may be on a
mount from another machine \(em and what crosses into the kernel is the
parameter sets and then the coded bytes of one frame at a time.
.PP
It checks every frame against the colour that frame was built with, and
that each display position is produced exactly once.
Both matter: the samples of
.B /lib/movies/test.mov
are in decode order and the stream has B-frames, so sample
.I n
is not frame
.IR n .
A test expecting otherwise fails against a decoder that is working
correctly \(em as this one did before it used the composition offsets.
.PP
Given a
.I file
argument it decodes that instead, taking the frame size from the track
and checking only what holds for any clip.
It skips reading the pixels back in that case: at 1080p a readback
costs as much as the decode, so timing one would measure this program
rather than the decoder.
.IR mkmovie ,
alongside the emu source, will build a larger clip to try it on.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/vstreamtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR quicktime (2),
.IR draw (3),
.IR drawdecodetest (1)
.SH BUGS
One short clip, one codec, one track.
Audio is not decoded at all, so nothing here tests synchronisation.
.PP
Frames are decoded as fast as the samples are written, with no regard
for the durations
.IR quicktime (2)
reports, so this measures decode throughput and not playback.
Measured that way, 1080p decodes at about 2.3ms a frame.
